<DocsLayout title="Host integration in Haft v9" description="Let haft init publish the current MCP config, skills, and managed instruction section for each supported coding-agent host.">
  <h1>Host integration</h1>

  <p>
    In v9, do not copy a generic prompt file across hosts. Run
    <code>haft init</code> for the exact host. Haft publishes that host's MCP
    configuration, transformed skills, and managed instruction section through
    one versioned init plan.
  </p>

  <h2>Stable hosts</h2>

  <pre><code>haft init --claude
haft init --codex
haft init --all      # full Claude + Codex integrations</code></pre>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Host</th>
        <th>MCP config</th>
        <th>Skills</th>
        <th>Instructions</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Claude Code</td>
        <td><code>.mcp.json</code></td>
        <td><code>~/.claude/skills/</code> or project <code>.claude/skills/</code></td>
        <td>Managed section in <code>CLAUDE.md</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Codex CLI / App</td>
        <td><code>.codex/config.toml</code></td>
        <td><code>~/.agents/skills/</code> or project <code>.agents/skills/</code></td>
        <td>Managed section in <code>AGENTS.md</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Use <code>--local</code> for project-local skill publication. Use
    <code>--mcp-only</code> only with an explicit host or <code>--all</code> when
    you intentionally want MCP config without skills or managed instructions.
    <code>--no-file-instructions</code> skips the instruction carrier while
    retaining the other selected host outputs.
  </p>

  <h2>Independent skill publication</h2>

  <pre><code>haft init --agents
haft init --agents --local
haft init --codex --agents</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>--agents</code> publishes only the shared agent skill bundle. It does
    not configure MCP or project instructions. When combined with
    <code>--codex</code>, the identical skill target is coalesced into one write.
  </p>

  <h2>Experimental and legacy adapters</h2>

  <p>
    Grok, Pi, Hermes, Zed, Antigravity, OpenCode, Cursor, Gemini CLI, and Air
    have explicit adapter flags. They do not all receive the same combination
    of MCP config, skills, and instruction files.
  </p>

  <pre><code>haft init --grok
haft init --pi
haft init --hermes
haft init --zed
haft init --agy
haft init --opencode
haft init --cursor
haft init --gemini
haft init --air</code></pre>

  <p>
    Read <code>haft init --help</code> from the installed release before
    scripting an adapter. Stable support in v9 is Claude Code and Codex; the
    other adapters retain their documented experimental or legacy boundaries.
  </p>

  <h2>Managed instruction safety</h2>

  <p>
    Re-init changes only the content between Haft markers in
    <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or <code>AGENTS.md</code>. Project text outside those
    markers remains project-owned. Recognized legacy Haft skills and slash
    commands are removed. A foreign file at a desired Haft-owned skill path
    blocks the write instead of being overwritten.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not maintain a second handwritten Haft protocol beside the managed
    section. Project-specific rules belong outside the markers; FPF and kernel
    behavior comes from the installed release.
  </p>

  <h2>Restart after every host change</h2>

  <p>
    Hosts cache tool schemas and skill manifests. Fully restart the host after
    init or upgrade, then check <code>haft version</code>, <code>haft doctor</code>,
    and the host's loaded MCP server. Editing a config file does not restart an
    already running process.
  </p>

